<h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ)</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Choose the correct option from (a), (b), (c), or (d).</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q1.</strong> An angle which is greater than  0^&deg;  and less than  90^&deg;  is called an: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Acute angle</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Obtuse angle</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Right angle</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Straight angle</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q2.</strong> If two angles are complementary, then the sum of their measures is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  180^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  90^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  360^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  45^&deg; </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q3.</strong> The complement of an angle measuring  35^&deg;  is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  55^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  145^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  65^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  135^&deg; </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q4.</strong> The supplement of an angle measuring  105^&deg;  is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  65^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  75^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  85^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  95^&deg; </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q5.</strong> Two angles forming a linear pair are always: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Complementary</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Vertically opposite</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Supplementary</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Equal</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q6.</strong> If two lines intersect at a point, then the vertically opposite angles are always: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Equal</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Unequal</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Complementary</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Supplementary</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q7.</strong> An angle is equal to its own supplement. What is the measure of this angle? </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  45^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  90^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  180^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  60^&deg; </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q8.</strong> If a transversal intersects two parallel lines, then the interior angles on the same side of the transversal are: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Equal</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Complementary</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Supplementary</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Reflex</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q9.</strong> An angle which is equal to its own complement is: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a)  30^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b)  45^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c)  60^&deg; </li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d)  90^&deg; </li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q10.</strong> If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, then the alternate interior angles are: </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Equal</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Unequal</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Complementary</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Supplementary</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section B: Fill in the Blanks</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Fill in the blanks with appropriate terms.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q11.</strong> The sum of the measures of two supplementary angles is _______ . </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q12.</strong> If two adjacent angles are supplementary, they form a _______ . </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q13.</strong> If two lines intersect at a point, the vertically opposite angles are always _______ . </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q14.</strong> Lines in a plane that do not intersect at any point are called _______ lines. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q15.</strong> A line that intersects two or more lines at distinct points is called a _______ . </div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section C: True / False</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: State whether the following statements are True or False.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q16.</strong> Two acute angles can form a linear pair. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q17.</strong> The sum of two complementary angles is  90^&deg; . </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q18.</strong> Vertically opposite angles are always equal in measure. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q19.</strong> Two obtuse angles can be supplementary to each other. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q20.</strong> If two parallel lines are cut by a transversal, each pair of corresponding angles are equal. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>True</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>False</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section D: Assertion-Reason</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Read the Assertion (A) and Reason (R) statements and choose the correct option.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q21.</strong> Assertion (A): The complement of an angle measuring  40^&deg;  is  50^&deg; .
Reason (R): The sum of two complementary angles is always  90^&deg; . </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q22.</strong> Assertion (A): Two acute angles can form a supplementary pair.
Reason (R): An acute angle is an angle whose measure is strictly less than  90^&deg; . </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q23.</strong> Assertion (A): If two parallel lines are intersected by a transversal, then alternate interior angles are equal.
Reason (R): Corresponding angles formed by a transversal with two parallel lines are equal. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q24.</strong> Assertion (A): If an angle measure is  60^&deg; , its linear pair partner measures  120^&deg; .
Reason (R): Angles forming a linear pair are supplementary, meaning their sum is  180^&deg; . </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true</li></ul><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q25.</strong> Assertion (A): Vertically opposite angles are equal only when the lines intersect perpendicular to each other.
Reason (R): When two lines intersect at any angle, the vertically opposite angles formed are always equal. </div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) A is true but R is false</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) A is false but R is true</li></ul><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section E: Very Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer in one or two sentences.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q26.</strong> Find the complement of an angle measuring  35^&deg; . </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q27.</strong> What is the measure of an angle which is equal to its supplement? </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q28.</strong> Define a linear pair of angles. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q29.</strong> If two lines intersect at a point and one pair of vertically opposite angles are acute, what type of angles are the other pair of vertically opposite angles? </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q30.</strong> State the condition for two lines intersected by a transversal to be parallel in terms of alternate interior angles. </div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section F: Short Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer in 30 to 50 words.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q31.</strong> An angle is greater than  45^&deg; . Is its complementary angle greater than  45^&deg; , equal to  45^&deg; , or less than  45^&deg; ? Explain your reasoning clearly. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q32.</strong> Find the measure of an angle which is  24^&deg;  more than its complement. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q33.</strong> Two lines intersect at a point  O . If one of the angles formed is  65^&deg; , find the measures of the remaining three angles. </div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: Diagram for Question 33]</div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q34.</strong> Two parallel lines  l  and  m  are cut by a transversal  t . If one of the interior angles on the same side of the transversal is  110^&deg; , find the measure of the other interior angle on the same side. </div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: Diagram for Question 34]</div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q35.</strong> An angle is equal to one-third of its supplement. Find the measure of the angle and its supplement. </div><h3 class='sec-banner' style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;padding-bottom:4px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Section G: Long Answer</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;margin-bottom:8px;page-break-after:avoid;'>Note: Answer in detail with diagrams, steps, or derivations where applicable.</p><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q36.</strong> In the given configuration, line  l  is parallel to line  m , and line  t  acts as a transversal. Two interior angles on the same side of the transversal are represented by  (2x + 15)^&deg;  and  (3x - 20)^&deg; .
(a) Solve for  x .
(b) Calculate the measure of each of these interior angles.
(c) Determine the measures of all eight angles formed at the intersections. </div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: Diagram for Question 36]</div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q37.</strong> Prove that if two lines intersect, then the sum of all four angles formed around the point of intersection is equal to  360^&deg; . Using this property, if one of the angles measures  72^&deg; , calculate the values of the other three angles with proper step-by-step geometric justification. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q38.</strong> In a geometry diagram, line  AB \parallel CD  and line  CD \parallel EF . A transversal line  PQ  intersects  AB ,  CD , and  EF  at points  X ,  Y , and  Z  respectively. Given that  \angle AXY = (5x + 10)^&deg;  and  \angle CZY = (2x + 40)^&deg; :
(a) Identify the relation between  \angle AXY  and  \angle CZY .
(b) Find the value of  x .
(c) Find the measures of  \angle AXY  and  \angle CYZ . </div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: Diagram for Question 38]</div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q39.</strong> Solve the following two-part problem regarding angle relationships:
(a) Two complementary angles are in the ratio  4 : 5 . Find the measure of each angle.
(b) Two supplementary angles are in the ratio  2 : 7 . Find the measure of each angle.
(c) Calculate the difference between the larger complementary angle and the smaller supplementary angle. </div><div class='q-item' style='margin-bottom:10px;page-break-inside:avoid;'><strong>Q40.</strong> In a figure, lines  p  and  q  are cut by a transversal  r .
(a) State three distinct criteria based on angle pairs that guarantee  p \parallel q .
(b) If an exterior angle on one intersection is  115^&deg;  and its corresponding interior angle on the same side is  (x + 25)^&deg; , test whether line  p  is parallel to line  q  when  x = 40^&deg; . Provide complete reasoning. </div><div style='background:#fef08a;color:#854d0e;padding:6px 12px;border-radius:4px;display:inline-block;font-weight:bold;margin:6px 0;'>[IMAGE NEEDED: Diagram for Question 40]</div>
